    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L09-ACTIVITY-017",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Write Your Own Equation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Whenever we have an equation with a radical symbol that contains a variable, we can solve it by isolating the radical and then raising each side of the equation to a power in order to get a new equation without radicals. Here is an…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L09-ACTIVITY-018",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Write Your Own Equation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "\\(\\displaystyle \\begin{align}\\text- 4 &= \\sqrt[3]{5p+1} \\\\ (\\text- 4)^3 &= \\left(\\sqrt[3]{5p+1}\\right)^3 \\\\ \\text- 64 &= 5p +1 \\\\ \\text- 65 &= 5p \\\\ \\text- 13 &= p \\end{align}\\)",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L09-ACTIVITY-019",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Write Your Own Equation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Sometimes this results in an equation with solutions that do not make the original equation true. If we use this strategy, it is good to check the solutions to the new equation we got after raising each side to a power, to be sure they…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
    {
      "SourceElementId": "G11-U03-L09-ACTIVITY-020",
      "SourceSection": "9.3: Write Your Own Equation",
      "SourceExcerpt": "Another way to solve these equations is to reason about what the answer is, instead of raising each side to a power. For example, if we are solving \\(\\sqrt{1-x}+5 = 11\\), we can rearrange it to get \\(\\sqrt{1-x}= 6\\) and then think, “If the…",
      "SourceLessonArtifact": "src/Edulytics.Core/Curriculum/LessonContent/RichV2/us-ccss-hs-alg2-u03-l09.rich-lesson-v2.json",
      "Treatment": "SOURCE_SCOPED_AUTHORING_WITH_TEACHER_REVIEW",
      "TestsExecuted": "NOT_RUN"
    },
